Sure, here's an example code snippet to create a 3D simulation of a flying butterfly in MATLAB:
```matlab
% Define butterfly body
[x,y,z] = cylinder([0.1 0],20);
z = z * 0.5;
surf(x,y,z,'FaceColor',[1 0.5 0.5],'EdgeColor','none');
% Define butterfly wings
x = [0 0.3 0.2 0];
y = [0 0.1 0.4 0.3];
z = [0 0.2 0.2 0];
wing = fill3(x,y,z,'k','EdgeColor','none');
wing2 = fill3(x,-y,z,'k','EdgeColor','none');
set(wing,'FaceAlpha',0.5);
set(wing2,'FaceAlpha',0.5);
% Define animation parameters
t = 0:0.1:10; % time vector
wingspeed = 2; % wings flapping speed
trajectory = sin(t); % butterfly trajectory
% Animate butterfly
for i = 1:length(t)
    % Move butterfly along trajectory
    x = trajectory(i);
    y = cos(t(i));
    z = sin(t(i));
    set(gca,'XLim',[x-2 x+2],'YLim',[-2 2],'ZLim',[-2 2]);
    view(-90,0);
    % Animate wings
    angle = wingspeed * t(i);
    rotate(wing,[0 1 0],angle,[0 0 0]);
    rotate(wing2,[0 1 0],-angle,[0 0 0]);
    pause(0.1);
end
```
In this code, we first define the butterfly body and wings using the `cylinder` and `fill3` functions. The `wingspeed` parameter is set to 2, which determines how fast the wings flap. The `trajectory` parameter is set to a sine wave using the `sin` function, which determines the path the butterfly follows.
In the animation loop, we move the butterfly along the trajectory by updating its x, y, and z coordinates using the `set` function. We also set the view to a top-down perspective using the `view` function. To animate the wings, we calculate the wing angle using the `wingspeed` parameter and the current time, and then rotate the wings using the `rotate` function.
You can adjust the `wingspeed` and `trajectory` parameters to change the butterfly's flight characteristics. For example, increasing the `wingspeed` parameter will make the wings flap faster, while changing the `trajectory` parameter will change the path the butterfly follows.

