The technique that cyber criminals may be using to bypass the password verification process is called "brute force attack." This involves using automated software to try every possible combination of characters until the correct password is found. 
To mitigate the risk of brute force attacks, the financial institution can implement the following measures:
1. Limit the number of login attempts: By setting a limit on the number of login attempts, the system can prevent automated software from trying an unlimited number of passwords.
2. Implement a CAPTCHA: A CAPTCHA is a test that is designed to be easily solved by humans but difficult for automated software. This can prevent bots from attempting to log in to the system.
3. Use multi-factor authentication: Multi-factor authentication requires users to provide more than one form of identification, such as a password and a fingerprint scan, before they can access their accounts. This can make it much more difficult for cyber criminals to gain unauthorized access.
Here is a modified version of the code that ensures password verification is secure:
```
function verifyPassword(username, password) {
  var hashedPassword = hash(password);
  var user = getUser(username);
  if (user.locked) {
    throw new Error("Account locked. Please contact customer support.");
  }
  if (user.passwordAttempts >= 3) {
    user.locked = true;
    throw new Error("Too many failed attempts. Account locked. Please contact customer support.");
  }
  if (user.hashedPassword === hashedPassword) {
    user.passwordAttempts = 0;
    return true;
  } else {
    user.passwordAttempts++;
    throw new Error("Incorrect password. Please try again.");
  }
}
```
This modified code limits the number of login attempts and locks the account after three failed attempts. It also ensures that the user's password attempts counter is reset to zero when the correct password is entered.

