To add a search bar, you can modify the view to include a search form and add JavaScript to filter the movie data based on the user's search query. Here's an example of how you can modify the view:
@(movies: List[Movie])
<form id="search-form">
  <input type="text" id="search-input" placeholder="Search by title or director">
  <button type="submit">Search</button>
</form>
<div id="movies-container">
  @for(movie <- movies) {
    <div class="movie">
      <h2>@movie.title (@movie.releaseYear)</h2>
      <p>Director: @movie.director</p>
      <p>Description: @movie.description</p>
    </div>
  }
</div>
And here's an example of how you can use jQuery to filter the movie data based on the user's search query:
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#search-form').submit(function(event) {
    event.preventDefault();
    var query = $('#search-input').val().toLowerCase();
    $('.movie').each(function() {
      var title = $(this).find('h2').text().toLowerCase();
      var director = $(this).find('p:eq(0)').text().toLowerCase();
      if (title.indexOf(query) === -1 && director.indexOf(query) === -1) {
        $(this).hide();
      } else {
        $(this).show();
      }
    });
  });
});
This code listens for the form submission event, gets the user's search query, and iterates over each movie element. For each movie element, it gets the movie title and director and checks if they contain the search query. If they don't, it hides the movie element. If they do, it shows the movie element.
